 socioeconomic classes  effective contraception includes not  only oral contraception but the  intelligent use of the diaphragm  the use of the intrauterine device and  in fact the intelligent use of the  method of periodic abstinence but i  think that uh from the response that  both of you made uh it's clear that uh  you have each weighed the the  equation of relative risk  and  benefit and come to the conclusion that  there is enough safety  in the use of oral contraceptions to  warrant the continuation of its uh use  in the uh in the country  uh so let us  focus now on the on the question of  these hazards and risks so that those in  the audience can make their own  judgments about the this balance of the  hazards on the one hand and the  effectiveness and usefulness on the  other i just respond first to  dr garcia's statement about women  wanting  100 protection i don't think all women  want 100 protection and i think this is  one of the reasons why there's a such a  tremendous variability in the reports in  the literature on the effectiveness of  such things as contents and diaphragms  i think the condom and the diaphragm  used compulsively  represents extraordinarily effective  techniques  but if you're casual about their use if  you uh  want a child or two sometime in the in  the future and don't really care all  that much whether it occurs a year from  now or  uh or two years from now  i think one has a different approach the  use of these mechanical devices than the  woman who has had four or five children  considers her family complete  and  is darn likely to be compulsive about  the use of these techniques  so that i i think in the interest of  individualizing our approach to these  women i think it would be unfortunate  to give the impression that every woman  one sees wants uh 100 perfection  furthermore if i can just complete my  thought  with the oral contraceptives one doesn't  have 100 percent protection  at least not with the sequential ones  which in my experience uh the number of  anecdotal reports i've come across  talking with patients with obstetricians  uh the failure rate is significant there  that protection is far from complete  well i i quite agree  that uh one has to individualize  however  there are again points that one might  take as issue here  uh  namely that the patient that has four or  five  pregnancies could likewise be  categorized as being in the upper age  group where she's going to fall within  the category of having greater risk of  taking the oral contraceptive so it's  not so simple matter and as a  gynecologist i don't know what your  experience is a clinical pharmacology  but as a gynecologist when i query my  patients they want an all or none sort  of reassurance  and very few of them elect to take  sequential modalities  can we pass now to the question of the  hazards themselves you pointed to dr  lasagna's uh  worldwide reputation as a clinical  pharmacologist so perhaps we should ask  him about some of these issues  that have been raised with respect to  the safety of the pill  let's take the cancer issue first where  does that stand and what are the real  facts in in that situation dr lasagna  i don't think there are a lot of heart  facts to go on here we have a long  background of experimental evidence  suggesting that hormones can be utilized  in  a number of animal models to produce  things like breast cancer that's clear  the relevance of these studies to the  use of these hormones uh in the variety  of clinical states in which they have  been used for a long time is unclear to  me  uh i think we're beginning to have  reports  on certain changes in  the cells of the cervix  that  make one at least  worry more than than  we've been worrying in the past and in  my  opinion at the moment the issue is an  open one uh  i think with the background that we have  on what these hormones can do in the  laboratory it would be  uh  foolish  to  assume that that we have no worries here  but i also would say that at the moment  the  uh evidence implicating these oral  contraceptives in the production of any  kind of cancer  is lacking  uh i think the evidence for example is  is much more compelling in regard to  traumatic disease  uh still for a moment on the cancer  issue uh on the basis of the animal work  isn't it more likely that the real  concern is the breast rather than the  cervix i think so yes  and uh have there been any uh clinical  observations or let me uh put it even  more fundamentally  has anyone actually done a study that  would give us some idea as to whether or  not there is a causal relationship i'm  not aware of any  it's it's a great pity i think that so  little attention has been paid  to the setting up of prospective studies  to monitor all these things that we're  concerned about and that we're we have a  hard time making definite statements  about because we're we're stuck with  retrospective analyses  you've been mostly involved with this  when attempts to set up prospective  studies have been uh  maybe at least the tries have been there  one of the greatest difficulties and one  of the things that  this is  new  to  most of the scientific world and  clinical  lines of investigation  that is to sample  a  denominator  that is as vast as is required to  measure  small changes in the numerator is indeed  a task that i don't know if it ever will  be accomplished or not with reference to  the oral contraceptive because there are  so many other factors that come into  the bias of selection and the like that  it's difficult to  do this and what's more  to try to have a truly controlled  population  that is not using the steroid  contraceptive versus a compassionate  theory concept was likewise very  difficult because the steroid  contraceptive group feel more compelled  because of the adverse ability to come  back to be seen more frequently  while those on other techniques  are much more difficult to follow and  this has been one of the greatest  pitfalls that we've had in trying to to  arrange for prospective studies i don't  know how to get around that well i think  that there are good epidemiologic  methods to account for this bias or to  take it into account in setting up the  design of a study  but  let me uh turn to another issue i think  the the one that has been most firmly  established among the possible serious  consequences of the use of oral  contraception has been uh the  epidemiologic studies and indications of  an increased risk  of thromboembolic disease associated  with the use of the drug studies both in  britain and now  on the basis of the recent report by the  advisory committee of citrix and  gynecology of the food and drug  administration confirmed here in the  united states  uh can you tell us how that stands and  what your view of that issue is dr  garcia  i i  that there is a  uh a strong  warning  that this presents  uh i don't know that this conclusively  proves  that there is a cause-and-effect  relationship and i think that as i  indicated previously epidemiological  views will not do this  they are still lacking many of the  mechanisms to explain  the  occurrence of these thromboembolic and  other effects  and until this gap has been bridged to  to demonstrate the mechanism of action  that will produce the effect  we still will not have a  cause-and-effect relationship but  nonetheless  these studies are very carefully  designed they're  been performed  according to  sound epidemiologic principles and  consistently although with different  variants of incidence and points of  comparison we have a concurrence with  all of the studies indicating an  association  between the use of oil contraceptives  and the occurrence of thrombophlebitis  and also consistently that this is seen  greater in the  older age group than in the younger age  i comment on the quality of the evidence  linking the contraceptives to oral  contraceptives to clotting  one thing i know something about is the  difficulty of linking drugs to to side  effects that are said to be associated  with them but in my opinion the evidence  on this point  is is so good  as to be  very compelling at least it's so much  better than we usually have for other  drugs that i think one can't ignore the  evidence the evidence exists in my  opinion not only in the form of  epidemiologic data but in the clotting  studies that have been done which in  almost all instances have shown a change  in women taking these pills in the  direction of increased clotting  the peculiar distribution of some of  these clots that are seen i mean women  coming in with with  clots in the veins of their arms the  most unusual phenomenon clots in the  vertebral arteries in young women a most  unusual neurological picture and then  the challenge cases the women who  develop recurrent thrombophlebitis each  time they're given the pill there aren't  a lot of those but there are enough i  think that to represent a legitimate  challenge  type of evidence  but this sort of bizarre type of  location of non-bombard phenomenon is  not new  it's just that it hasn't been focused  attention on in association with the use  of oral contraceptive number one and  secondly we have to also uh consider  that uh the  uh  i'm afraid not garcia i'm not gonna let  you finish your thought because our time  is up  uh  in a few seconds left to us i would like  to attempt a brief summary of this  discussion  it seems to me that we have reached a  consensus  that  this is a type of medication that does  involve risk  both participants agree that the risk is  not so great to warrant removal of these  drugs from the physician's armamentarium  i think that both agree also however  that when these drugs are used they must  be used under very careful supervision  and although the point wasn't raised  i feel confident that both would agree  that the  the labeling instructions required by  the food and drug administration should  be adhered to very strictly  as with all drugs there is a question of  relative risk versus relative advantage  in determining safety  on that basis and by that definition it  seems that there is an element of safety  sufficient for continuation of the use  of oral contraceptives  we thank dr salsa ramon garcia dr louis  lasagna and dr sheldon siegel for their  interesting analysis of a critical  problem in patient care  in subsequent programs we shall continue  to record equally significant concepts  and controversies in modern medicine  the opinions expressed on this program  do not necessarily constitute  endorsement by the department of health  education and welfare the public health  service or its constituents  you
 

